## Netlists:  an Apollo netlist, read into nodes, ports and connections
// A netlist file is ONE component, `{ type, attrs, ports?, connections?, subcomponents? }`, each subcomponent the same.
// These templates are the public face;  `netlistReader.ts`, beside them, reads the JSON into them, fast.
// A connection is the text "a.port b.port", declared by the node that holds both ends:
// "a" is one of its children, and a bare "port" is the node's own.

a netlist is a thing where:
	- it has a name as text
	- it has a revision as text
	- it has a source url as text
	- it has a source file as text
	- it has a root as a netlist node
	- its "nodes" is a list of netlist nodes
	- its "connections" is a list of netlist connections
	- its "problems" is a list of netlist problems
	- its "node count" is the number of items in its nodes
	- its "connection count" is the number of items in its connections
	- its "problem count" is the number of items in its problems
	- it "has problems" if its problem count > 0

a netlist node is a thing where:
	- it has a name as text
	- it has a kind as text
	- it has a path as text
	- it has a depth as a number
	- it has attrs as an object
	- it has a parent as a netlist node
	- it has a subnetlist as text
	- its "children" is a list of netlist nodes
	- its "ports" is a list of netlist ports
	- its "connections" is a list of netlist connections
	- its "child count" is the number of items in its children
	- its "port count" is the number of items in its ports
	- its "connection count" is the number of items in its connections
	- it "is a leaf" if its children is empty
	- it "is the root" if its depth is 0
	- it "is external" if its subnetlist is not empty

a netlist port is a thing where:
	- it has a name as text
	- it has a kind as text
	- it has a direction as text
	- it has attrs as an object
	- it has a node as a netlist node
	- its "connections" is a list of netlist connections
	- its "path" is the path of its node + "." + its name
	- it "is an input" if its direction is "in"
	- it "is an output" if its direction is "out"
	- it "is connected" if the number of items in its connections > 0

a netlist connection is a thing where:
	- it has text as text
	- it has an owner as a netlist node
	- it has a from-end as a netlist endpoint
	- it has a to-end as a netlist endpoint
	- it "is resolved" if the port of its from-end is defined and the port of its to-end is defined

a netlist endpoint is a thing where:
	- it has text as text
	- it has a node as a netlist node
	- it has a port as a netlist port
	- it "is a bare port" if its text does not include "."

a netlist problem is a thing where:
	- it has a kind as text
	- it has a message as text
	- it has a path as text
	- it has text as text
